WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:02.710
JAN KLEINERT: Key
Visualizer for Cloud Spanner

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:04.720
is a tool in the
Cloud Console that

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.170
helps developers and operations
teams analyze Spanner usage

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.490
patterns.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.040
It generates visual
reports for your databases,

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.990
broken down by ranges
of rows over time,

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:17.800
and it provides insights
into usage patterns at scale

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.890
that are difficult to
understand otherwise.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:22.300
I'm going to walk
you through how

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.390
to use Key Visualizer to look
at usage patterns for a Spanner

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:29.260
database that's used by an
application I'm working on.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.360
I've recently inherited
the responsibility

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.050
for this application, and I'm
not very familiar with it yet.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.440
First, let's get acquainted with
the Key Visualizer interface.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.740
When you access Key
Visualizer, you'll see a scan.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.250
The core of a Key Visualizer
scan is the heatmap.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.340
The heatmap shows us the
value of a metric over time,

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.190
broken down into
ranges of row keys.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.890
The x-axis represents
time, and the y-axis

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.410
represents row ranges.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:00.430
Low values are cold, represented
by darker colors like purple,

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.560
and high values are
hot, represented

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.890
by yellow or bright white
for the highest values.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.510
The different patterns
in the heatmap

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:11.260
can help diagnose
issues at a glance.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.450
Each row range represents
a contiguous set

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:16.030
of rows in a single
table or index.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:20.500
A row range is defined
by a start and end key.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.360
Within each table
or index, keys are

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.460
sorted by primary key values.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.020
At the top of the
heatmap, there's

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.420
a key for the heatmap colors.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.490
As you hover over a
cell in the heatmap,

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.850
you'll see details about
the value, timestamp, table,

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:35.650
and key bucket.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:39.220
You can select a portion
of the heatmap to zoom in.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.900
You can also select a time
range using the date picker.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.290
You can pan around the heatmap
by clicking and dragging.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.050
You can use the select box
to change between metrics.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.390
When I look at the graph
for number of rows read,

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.150
I can see that the reads
for keys in the Albums table

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.400
are distributed fairly
evenly across row ranges.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.100
However, for the
Singers table, it's

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.800
yellow or hot across the
board, and especially

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.780
in the beginning row ranges.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.639
After looking at my
application code,

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:10.680
I notice the
query-singers-last name

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.910
function is called frequently.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:14.550
I can see that we'd
probably benefit

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.550
from having a secondary
index on the LastName field,

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.110
so that Spanner
doesn't have to scan

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:22.680
the entire table every time
query-singers-last name is

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:23.760
called.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.830
We can do this with a
CREATE INDEX statement

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.780
or by using force index.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.910
I'll make the change
and see what happens.

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:32.500
With the application
change deployed,

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.330
we can see that the
number of rows read

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:37.540
has dropped dramatically
for the Singers table.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.480
Seeing a single hot table
is just one common pattern

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.012
you may uncover
with Key Visualizer.

00:02:43.012 --> 00:02:45.220
Here are some of the other
patterns you may encounter

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:47.470
and what issues
they might indicate.

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:49.390
A heatmap with a
single bright diagonal

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.430
line or a triangle
with a flat top

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:55.240
can indicate a database
that uses sequential keys.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.120
Sequential keys are an
antipattern in Spanner,

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.020
and you can learn more about
why sequential keys cause

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.110
hotspots and alternatives in the
documentation on schema design

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.490
best practices.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:09.880
A heatmap with horizontal
bands of bright color separated

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.270
by dark colors indicate
that specific row ranges

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:17.550
are consistently being more
heavily accessed over time.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.140
To address this,
you can consider

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.210
changing your schema
design or your application

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.300
so that the data in
a heavily used row

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.350
or in an excessively large row
is spread across multiple rows.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.080
A heatmap with multiple
diagonal bright lines,

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.330
either rising or
falling, usually

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.100
indicates sequential
reads or writes

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.320
to rows in a short
period of time.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.380
If the lines correspond with
read activity, such as a table

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.360
export, no action
is usually required.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.180
If you're experiencing
performance issues

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.160
during these reads,
you can consider

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.200
increasing the number
of nodes for instance

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.330
during those time periods.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.580
If the lines correspond
with write activity

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.410
and you're experiencing
performance issues,

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.710
you should consider changing
your workload to a random write

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.800
instead of a sequential write.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.330
A heatmap showing
bright horizontal bands

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.670
that appear partway
across the heatmap

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.760
indicate a sudden change in the
usage pattern of a database.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.450
This could be a rogue query
or an unexpected surge

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.340
in activity for certain rows.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:17.010
You can learn even more
about exploring heatmaps

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.920
and understanding heatmap
patterns in the Spanner Key

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.079
Visualizer documentation.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:25.050
In this video, we've looked at
how Key Visualizer for Spanner

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.480
can help you analyze usage
patterns in your Spanner

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:28.860
databases.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.320
This tool adds to
existing monitoring tools,

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.600
such as query stats and
monitoring in the Cloud

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.750
Console, to help simplify
the process of monitoring

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.210
and troubleshooting
performance issues.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.930
In the description
below the video,

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:44.310
you'll find links to the Key
Visualizer documentation.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:47.490
Key Visualizer is now available
for all Spanner databases,

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.910
so start exploring.

